<header><p class="st-kicker">city state · Mesopotamia and Iran</p><h1 class="st-title">Hamazi</h1><p class="st-meta">2500 BCE – 2400 BCE (approx.) · capital Hamazi</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A shadowy early kingdom in the hills east of the Tigris, listed among the holders of kingship in the Sumerian King List and in contact with distant Ebla.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Hamazi is known only from a handful of texts, and even its location is uncertain; most scholars place it in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zagros-mountains">Zagros</a> foothills between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/diyala-river">Diyala</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/little-zab">Lesser Zab</a>, perhaps near modern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sulaymaniyah">Sulaymaniyah</a>. It appears in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/early-dynastic-period">Early Dynastic period</a>, around the 25th century BCE.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sumerian-king-list">Sumerian King List</a> records a single king, Hadanish, as holding kingship for Hamazi before it passed to <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/uruk">Uruk</a>. A letter from the archives of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/ebla">Ebla</a> in <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/syrian-arab-republic">Syria</a>, sent to a ruler named Zizi of Hamazi, shows that the kingdom was part of a long-distance diplomatic network and exchanged gifts with Syrian courts. Nothing is known of its society, language or religion beyond these mentions.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>Hamazi disappears from the sources by the Akkadian period, presumably absorbed by stronger neighbours or by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/akkadian-empire">Akkadian Empire's</a> campaigns in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zagros-mountains">Zagros</a>. A late <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/third-dynasty-of-ur">Ur III</a> text mentions people of Hamazi, but no later state of the name is known.</p><p class="st-conf">Few sources survive for this state; details are uncertain.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/hamazi.jpg" alt="Hamazi" width="320" height="303"><figcaption>Image: Hilprecht, H. V. (Hermann Vollrat), 1859-1925 · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Uhub_vase_inscription.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>2500 BCE – 2400 BCE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/26th-century-bce">26th century BCE</a> – <a href="/centuries/25th-century-bce">25th century BCE</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>city state</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>tribal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Hamazi</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Hadanish, Zizi</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/iran">Iran</a>, <a href="/countries/iraq">Iraq</a></dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=-2450&amp;focus=hamazi" aria-label="Hamazi on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="6082 1006 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Hamazi lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M6402 1262h0"/><path class="map-dot-ap" d="M6402 1262h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>Location approximate: shown in present-day Iran. <a href="/atlas?year=-2450&amp;focus=hamazi">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>

